"[2509.15207v1] FlowRL: Matching Reward Distributions for LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15207v1)\nciteturn3375view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15207v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 18 Sep 2025 (this version), latest version 4 Nov 2025 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:FlowRL: Matching Reward Distributions for LLM Reasoning\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xuekai Zhu , cite7†Daixuan Cheng , cite8†Dinghuai Zhang , cite9†Hengli Li , cite10†Kaiyan Zhang , cite11†Che Jiang , cite12†Youbang Sun , cite13†Ermo Hua , cite14†Yuxin Zuo , cite15†Xingtai Lv , cite16†Qizheng Zhang , cite17†Lin Chen , cite18†Fanghao Shao , cite19†Bo Xue , cite20†Yunchong Song , cite21†Zhenjie Yang , cite22†Ganqu Cui , cite23†Ning Ding , cite24†Jianfeng Gao , cite25†Xiaodong Liu , cite26†Bowen Zhou , cite27†Hongyuan Mei , cite28†Zhouhan Lin L12: View a PDF of the paper titled FlowRL: Matching Reward Distributions for LLM Reasoning, by Xuekai Zhu and 22 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite29†View PDF cite30†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:We propose FlowRL: matching the full reward distribution via flow balancing instead of maximizing rewards in large language model (LLM) reinforcement learning (RL). Recent advanced reasoning models adopt reward-maximizing methods (\\eg, PPO and GRPO), which tend to over-optimize dominant reward signals while neglecting less frequent but valid reasoning paths, thus reducing diversity.\nL16: In contrast, we transform scalar rewards into a normalized target distribution using a learnable partition function, and then minimize the reverse KL divergence between the policy and the target distribution. We implement this idea as a flow-balanced optimization method that promotes diverse exploration and generalizable reasoning trajectories.\nL17: We conduct experiments on math and code reasoning tasks: FlowRL achieves a significant average improvement of $10.0\\%$ over GRPO and $5.1\\%$ over PPO on math benchmarks, and performs consistently better on code reasoning tasks. These results highlight reward distribution-matching as a key step toward efficient exploration and diverse reasoning in LLM reinforcement learning.\nL18: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite31†arXiv:2509.15207 [cs.LG]\nL20:    | (or cite32†arXiv:2509.15207v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL21:    | cite33†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.15207†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL22: ## Submission history\nL23: \nL24: From: Xuekai Zhu [cite34†view email ]\nL25: [v1] Thu, 18 Sep 2025 17:56:36 UTC (377 KB)\nL26: cite35†[v2] Tue, 30 Sep 2025 07:25:00 UTC (381 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v3] Tue, 4 Nov 2025 08:52:02 UTC (381 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled FlowRL: Matching Reward Distributions for LLM Reasoning, by Xuekai Zhu and 22 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite29†View PDF L36:   * cite30†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite36†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite37†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite38†< prev |   cite39†next > L45: \nL46: cite40†new | cite41†recent | cite42†2025-09 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite43†cs L51: cite44†cs.AI L52: cite45†cs.CL L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite46†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite47†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite48†Seman